---
layout: article
title: Bird email
description: Send emails to your Appwrite users through Bird's email API and Appwrite Messaging.
back: /docs/
---
[Bird](https://bird.com) lets you send customized email messages to your users.
These emails can be sent immediately or scheduled.
You can send emails for purposes like reminders, promotions, announcements, and even custom authentication flows.

The Bird email provider sends through Bird's HTTP email API with a Bird API key, so it doesn't need SMTP settings. To send SMS through Bird too, add a [Bird SMS](/docs/products/messaging/bird-sms) provider next to it.

{% section #add-provider step=1 title="Add provider" %}

To add Bird as an email provider, navigate to **Messaging** > **Providers** > {% icon icon="plus" size="m" /%} **Add provider**.
{% only_dark %}
![Add a Bird provider](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/dark/add-bird.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Add a Bird provider](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/add-bird.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Bird is listed under both **Email** and **SMS**. Choose **Bird** under **Email** to open the **Configure Bird** step.
{% /section %}
{% section #configure-provider step=2 title="Configure provider" %}

In the **Configure Bird** step, you need a Bird API key and a sender on a verified domain. On Appwrite Cloud, you can let Appwrite create the API key for you with one-click setup, or fill in the fields by hand.

{% only_dark %}
![Configure Bird email provider](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/dark/configure-bird-email.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Configure Bird email provider](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/configure-bird-email.avif)
{% /only_light %}

## One-click setup {% #one-click-setup %}

On Appwrite Cloud, the **Configure Bird** step opens with a **One-click setup** card above the form.

1. Click **Connect with Bird** and authorize Appwrite in Bird. You are returned to this step with your Bird workspace connected.
1. Select a verified **Domain**. Domains that Bird hasn't verified yet are listed but can't be selected. If none are verified, click **Manage domains**, verify one in Bird, and click **Check again**.
1. Click **Generate API key**.
1. Review the fields and click **Create provider**.

{% only_dark %}
![One-click setup card with a verified Bird domain selected](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/dark/one-click-bird-email.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![One-click setup card with a verified Bird domain selected](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/one-click-bird-email.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Appwrite creates an API key in your Bird workspace named `Appwrite SMTP: <project name>` with only the `emails:write` scope, and fills it into the **API key** field. Bird keys aren't tied to a domain, so the domain you pick sets the sender address. Appwrite also fills any empty fields: the **Sender email** becomes `noreply@<domain>` unless the one you typed is already on that domain, the **Sender name** defaults to your project name, and the provider **Name** defaults to `One-click Bird sender`.

If you leave the wizard without creating the provider, Appwrite revokes the key it generated so it doesn't stay unused in your Bird workspace. Clicking **Generate API key** again replaces the key in the form and revokes the previous one.

The Bird connection lives on your Console account and is shared with the [Bird SMS provider](/docs/products/messaging/bird-sms#one-click-setup) and the [one-click SMTP setup](/docs/products/auth/message-templates#smtp-one-click-setup) in project settings. Connecting once covers all three.

## Configure manually {% #configure-manually %}

You will need to provide the following information from your **Bird dashboard**.

{% table %}
* Field name
*
---
* Name
* A name for the provider in Appwrite.
---
* API key
* Head to **Platform tools** > **API keys** and create a key with the `emails` scope at the `write` level. Bird keys start with `bk_` and your region, for example `bk_eu1_`, and Appwrite uses that prefix to send to the right regional API. See [Bird's authentication guide](https://bird.com/docs/guides/authentication).
---
* Sender email
* The provider sends emails from this address. It must be on a domain verified under **Email** > **Domains** in Bird. See [Bird's sending domains guide](https://bird.com/docs/guides/email/sending-domains).
---
* Sender name
* The sender name that appears in the emails sent from this provider.
{% /table %}

After adding the details, click **Create provider**. The provider is enabled once it has both an API key and a sender email. To set a reply-to name and email, open the provider from **Messaging** > **Providers** after it's created, or pass `replyToName` and `replyToEmail` through the API.

## How Bird email delivery works {% #delivery %}

- **One message per recipient.** Each recipient in **To** gets a message of their own, with any CC and BCC addresses copied on each. Appwrite sends several recipients to Bird in batches of up to 100.
- **Transactional category.** Messages are sent with Bird's `transactional` category, so Bird delivers them as written, without adding an unsubscribe link.
- **Size limit.** A message can be at most 20 MB, counting its body and its attachments after base64 encoding, which makes about 15 MB of attachments. Larger messages fail without reaching Bird.
- **Per-recipient errors.** If Bird refuses a recipient in a batch, Appwrite retries that batch one message at a time, so only the refused recipient fails. Bird refuses placeholder domains such as `example.com` and `test.com`, so use real addresses or [Bird's sandbox addresses](https://bird.com/docs/guides/email/testing-sandbox), such as `delivered@messagebird.dev`, for test targets.
{% /section %}

{% section #test-provider step=3 title="Test provider" %}
Before sending your first message,
make sure you've configured [a topic](/docs/products/messaging/topics) and [a target](/docs/products/messaging/targets) to send messages to.
{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #console title="Console" %}
To send a test message, navigate to **Messaging** > **Messages** > {% icon icon="plus" size="m" /%} **Create message** > **Email**.
{% only_dark %}
![Create email message](/images/docs/messaging/messages/dark/create-email-message.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create email message](/images/docs/messaging/messages/create-email-message.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Add your message and in the targets step, select one of your test targets. Set the schedule to **Now** and click **Send**.

Verify that you can receive the message in your inbox. If not, check for logs in the Appwrite Console or in your provider's logs.
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #server-sdk title="Server SDK" %}
To send a message programmatically, use an [Appwrite Server SDK](/docs/sdks#server).
{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

// Init SDK
const client = new sdk.Client();

const messaging = new sdk.Messaging(client);

client
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>') // Your project ID
    .setKey('<API_KEY>') // Your secret API key
;

const message = await messaging.createEmail({
  messageId: '<MESSAGE_ID>',
  subject: '<SUBJECT>',
  content: '<CONTENT>'
});
```
```deno
import * as sdk from "npm:node-appwrite";

